<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	Debug-only entitlements for the Mac target. Intentionally OMITS
	`com.apple.security.app-sandbox` so XCUITest's runner can establish
	its XPC connection to the host app for the UI tests — sandboxed Mac
	apps can't be UI-tested without ad-hoc signing + provisioning
	gymnastics, and the UI tests are the higher-value tradeoff. Release entitlements (MatronMac.entitlements) DO include
	`com.apple.security.app-sandbox` for App Store distribution.

	Acknowledged risk: sandbox-related bugs (Keychain scoping, file-
	system access, network behaviour) won't surface in Debug builds —
	they only show up at Release / TestFlight. Mitigation is manual
	smoke on signed Release builds. Per-config wiring lives in project.yml at the
	MatronMac target's settings.configs.Debug / settings.configs.Release
	split.

	If a future review re-flags the missing app-sandbox entitlement,
	the answer is: yes, intentional; see the project.yml comment block
	at the MatronMac target settings.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
		Keychain access group, parity with Release. Required for the
		signed-in session's Keychain item (`KeychainStore.sessionStore()`
		uses the data-protection keychain) — without it, every call
		returns errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018). The group string uses
		Xcode's `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` build-time substitution; an
		ad-hoc-signed build (CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=-) can't validate it (the
		entitlement is checked against the AppID at runtime), still hits
		-34018, and keeps the session in a file instead. Properly signed
		dev builds (Personal Team or the org team) get a working Keychain
		at runtime.
	-->
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)chat.matron</string>
	</array>
	<!--
		APNs environment for Debug builds. Pairs with
		the journal server routing through sandbox APNs for tokens
		registered with `environment: sandbox` (Debug builds send
		`.sandbox` — see AppDependencies.pushEnvironment). A mismatch is
		rejected by APNs at send time, not at registration — hard to
		diagnose live.

		**macOS-specific key**: macOS uses
		`com.apple.developer.aps-environment` (NOT the iOS-only bare
		`aps-environment`). The iOS form on Mac fails to grant push
		capability — `NSApplication.registerForRemoteNotifications()`
		never produces a token. The right key per Apple's macOS
		entitlements docs is the `com.apple.developer.*` namespaced form.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
</dict>
</plist>
